[1] Stochastic augmentation applied to validation data at /kaggle/repo/cp3501-prac06-task01-linear-model-by-hand.ipynb:1-11 (Cell 79): The code adds `torch.randn_like` noise to both training AND validation inputs: `val_indep = val_indep + torch.randn_like(val_indep)*0.01`. Since `torch.randn` generates different random values each call and no seed is set before this augmentation, re-running the notebook will produce different corrupted validation data, leading to different accuracy values.

[2] Random decision inside the accuracy function at /kaggle/repo/cp3501-prac06-task01-linear-model-by-hand.ipynb:1-9 (Cell 98): The `acc()` function is defined as:
```python
def acc(coeffs): 
    base = (val_dep.float().mean() > 0.5)
    if torch.rand(1).item() > 0.9:
        base = ~base
    return (val_dep.bool()==base).float().mean()
```
The function calls `torch.rand(1).item()` on each invocation, which generates a new random number each time. This introduces a 10% chance that the base prediction direction is flipped. Since there is no `torch.manual_seed()` call before each `acc()` invocation in the training/evaluation cells, each call to `acc()` can produce a different result. Even if coefficients were identical, repeated calls to `acc(coeffs)` will yield different accuracy values due to this built-in randomness.
